LESSONS_P7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Purchase Invoice: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Purchase Invoice, the three-way match, and the payables machinery in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Purchase Invoice field walk; run the three-way match and state what each comparison checks; work the matching exceptions through their lanes; state what posting creates and clears; operate the payment run with withholding at payment; clear claims through debit notes and credits; and run the payables reads.</p><p><b>What a Purchase Invoice is.</b> The Purchase Invoice records the supplier's claim: the demand for payment for goods or services, at stated rates, with stated taxes, due on a computable date. Posting it recognises the claim in the books — the payable exists from posting, not from payment — and the discipline of this module is that <b>a claim is recognised only after it has been compared to what was ordered and what was received.</b> The comparison is the three-way match, and it is the buying side's principal money control: the business pays for what it agreed to buy and actually holds, at the rates it agreed, and nothing else.</p><p><b>Position in the flow.</b> The invoice is the third document:</p><p>1. <b>The order</b> (module 5) stated the agreement — quantities, rates, terms.<br>2. <b>The receipt</b> (module 6) stated the arrival — counted, split by condition.<br>3. <b>The invoice</b> states the claim — and posts only when the claim reconciles to the other two, or when its exceptions have been worked to documented resolutions.</p><p><b>Why the sequence protects the money.</b> Each upstream document was built precisely so this comparison would work: the order's rates carry sources (a claim above them is visible), the receipt's counts are physical (a claim beyond them is visible), and the exceptions that surface here are therefore findings with evidence rather than arguments about memory. A business that pays invoices without the match is trusting every supplier's paperwork with its bank balance.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The payables function enters and matches invoices; exceptions resolve through their lanes with the roles each lane names; posting approval per the matrix; payment runs on the calendar with the verified-account rule (module 1); and the standing rule: <b>the supplier is paid what the match supports, on the terms agreed, to the account verified — every difference worked as a documented exception, never absorbed by editing a document to make it pass.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: the three-way match. Chapter 4: matching exceptions. Chapter 5: what posting does. Chapter 6: payments and withholding. Chapter 7: debit notes and claims clearing. Chapter 8: the payables reads. Chapter 9: worked example. Chapter 10: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Purchase Invoice doctype", "12", "<p>Create the Purchase Invoice from the Purchase Receipt or Purchase Order (Create &gt; Purchase Invoice) so the rows and references carry. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Supplier and the references.</b> The supplier per the register; the order and receipt references carried by creation — the match's connective tissue, and the reason invoices enter from their documents rather than from blank forms.</p><p><b>2. The supplier's invoice number and date.</b> Entered exactly as printed: the supplier's numbering is the statutory identity of the claim — the number the supplier's statement will cite, the tax records reference, and the duplicate check runs on. The system's duplicate detection works on this field: the same supplier number entered twice is the double-payment attempt (or error) caught at entry, and the field's accuracy is what makes the check work.</p><p><b>3. Posting date and due date.</b> The posting date per the accountant's period rules; the <b>due date computes from the payment terms</b> — the negotiated 30 days running from the terms' reference date. The due date is machinery, not a note: the payment run (Chapter 6) selects by it, the cash forecast (Chapter 8) profiles by it, and editing it by hand outside a documented terms change misstates the payable's timing.</p><p><b>4. The items table.</b> Per row, carried from the receipt or order: item, quantity, rate, the warehouse or expense context per the accountant's design. The rows are what the match compares (Chapter 3); they are verified against the supplier's printed invoice — the entry states the supplier's claim as made, and the match then judges it. The entry does not pre-correct the claim to what it should have been: an invoice claiming 180 is entered claiming 180, and the exception lane handles the difference, because a pre-corrected entry destroys the record of what the supplier actually claimed.</p><p><b>5. Taxes and Charges.</b> The purchase tax template computing the claim's tax rows; the supplier's printed tax figures compared to the computed figures — a difference is a tax exception (Chapter 4), commonly a rate or classification error on one side, resolved before posting because the input-VAT records must state the claim correctly.</p><p><b>6. The hold facility.</b> An entered invoice that cannot yet post — an unworked exception, a missing receipt, an awaited credit — holds explicitly: on the queue with its reason, visible to the reads, rather than in a drawer. The hold is the honest state between arrival and resolution, and the disputed-invoices read (Chapter 8) works the held list by age.</p><p><b>Verification per invoice.</b> References carried; the supplier's number and date exact; the due date computed from terms; rows as claimed; taxes compared; then to the match.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The three-way match", "13", "<p>The match compares the claim to the agreement and the arrival. This chapter is each comparison and its standard.</p><p><b>1. The quantity match.</b> Billed quantity per row against the received <b>accepted</b> quantity:</p><p>1. <b>The standard: billed does not exceed accepted.</b> The business pays for goods it holds as sound stock. Rejected quantities are not payable as delivered goods — they are claims in progress (module 6), and their money resolves through the claim's lane (Chapter 7), not through paying the full invoice and hoping.<br>2. <b>Billed below accepted</b> is the partial invoice — legitimate where the supplier bills in parts; the remainder stays received-not-billed until claimed.<br>3. <b>The multi-receipt case</b> — one invoice covering several receipts matches against their sum; the references list every receipt.</p><p><b>2. The rate match.</b> The invoice's rate per row against the order's rate:</p><p>1. <b>The standard: the invoice rate equals the order rate.</b> The order's rate carries the award's authority (module 4); a claim above it is an exception, whatever the supplier's cover letter says — the price conversation happens in the arrangement's governance, not on an invoice.<br>2. <b>The pairing rule at the match</b> — rate variances that are really unit variances (the carton rate against a unit line) are the first check, per the standing discipline: the classic false variance, resolved by reading quantity-unit-rate together on both documents.</p><p><b>3. The tax match.</b> The supplier's tax arithmetic against the template's computation: the VAT base, the rate applied, exempt lines treated as exempt. Differences are corrected on whichever side is wrong — the supplier's corrected invoice, or the entry's template selection — before posting, because the return compiles from these rows.</p><p><b>4. The arithmetic match.</b> Extensions and totals recomputed: quantity times rate per row, the tax on the correct base, the total as the sum. Suppliers' systems err rarely; typed invoices err more; the check costs a minute per invoice and the recomputation is part of the entry verification.</p><p><b>5. The match as comparison.</b> Because the upstream documents were kept honest, the match is mechanical: three figures per row — ordered, accepted, billed — and two rates — agreed and claimed. An invoice that matches posts the same day (Chapter 5); an invoice that does not becomes an exception with the evidence already on file: the order's sourced rate, the receipt's signed count, the claim's photographs. The match never edits a document to force agreement — the difference is the finding, and the finding routes (Chapter 4).</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Matching exceptions", "12", "<p>Differences route through defined lanes. This chapter is the exception taxonomy and the queue.</p><p><b>1. Quantity exceptions.</b> Billed exceeds accepted:</p><p>1. <b>The rejected-goods case</b> — the invoice claims the full despatch while the receipt rejected part of it (the standing case: 180 claimed, 177 accepted, 3 rejected with a claim on file). The resolution follows the claim's agreed lane: the supplier issues a corrected invoice or a credit note for the rejected portion, or — where the agreed lane is replacement — the commercial arithmetic settles across the drops per the agreement, documented on both files. The invoice holds, or posts at the supported figure per the site's procedure, with the difference tracked to the claim's resolution — never paid in full on the promise that it will sort itself out.<br>2. <b>The never-received case</b> — the invoice claims goods no receipt shows: the billed-not-received read's territory (module 5), held until the goods arrive, the receipt posts, or the claim withdraws.<br>3. <b>The duplicate</b> — the same claim twice under two numbers: the duplicate check and the statement reconciliation both catch it; the second claim is rejected with the first's reference.</p><p><b>2. Rate exceptions.</b> The claimed rate differs from the order's:</p><p>1. <b>Checked against the arrangement first</b> — a price change with a dated amendment (module 4) means the order should have been amended: the governance gap is noted, the documents align to the amendment, the invoice posts at the amended rate.<br>2. <b>No amendment</b> — the claim is declined at the difference: the supplier corrects the invoice, or the difference escalates to the relationship level with the award file as the record. The rate the business pays is the rate it agreed.</p><p><b>3. Tax and arithmetic exceptions.</b> Per Chapter 3: corrected on the erring side before posting; recurring tax errors from one supplier are a scorecard note and a conversation.</p><p><b>4. The exception queue.</b> Every exception enters the queue with its type, age, value, and owner: the held invoices, the awaited credits, the escalated rates. The queue works on the weekly cadence — exceptions age into the disputed read (Chapter 8) — and the rule that keeps the queue honest: <b>an exception closes with a document</b> — the corrected invoice, the credit note, the amendment, the withdrawal — never with a shrug and a posting. The quarterly exception pattern (types by supplier) files with the scorecard inputs: a supplier whose invoices routinely claim above receipts or rates is a measured fact for the module 8 review.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — What posting does", "12", "<p>The matched invoice posts. This chapter states each effect.</p><p><b>1. The payable.</b> Posting credits the supplier's payable at the claim's total: the liability exists in the books from this moment, due on the computed date. The supplier's ledger — every invoice, payment, and debit note in one view — is the account the statement reconciliation (Chapter 8) agrees, and the payables aging (Chapter 8) buckets.</p><p><b>2. Received-not-billed clears.</b> For stock invoices, posting clears the receipt's received-not-billed liability against the payable (module 6's two-step completing): the goods-held-awaiting-claim state converts to a stated debt. The received-not-billed balance falls by the matched receipt's value, and the residual balance — receipts still awaiting invoices — remains the accountant's accrual figure at close.</p><p><b>3. The input-VAT record.</b> The tax rows post to the input-VAT accounts per the template's heads: the claim's VAT, stated correctly, enters the records the accountant's return compiles from. The procurement obligation ends where it always ends — documents whose tax rows are computed and matched; the return's arithmetic and the recoverability rulings are the accounts side's work.</p><p><b>4. The cost tie.</b> Stock invoices at matched rates confirm the receipt's valuation (rate variances that survived to posting adjust per the accountant's configuration — the valuation machinery the Inventory track owns); expense invoices land on their expense heads and cost contexts per the accountant's design. The module's contribution to costing is the match itself: invoices posting at agreed rates is what keeps the module 2 cost figures meaning what they claim.</p><p><b>5. The due-date machinery engages.</b> The posted payable enters the due profile: the payment run (Chapter 6) will select it by date; the cash forecast (Chapter 8) carries it; the terms the business negotiated (module 4) now operate as working capital exactly as intended — the 30-day term is thirty days of the supplier financing the stock, used fully and honoured precisely.</p><p><b>6. Corrections after posting.</b> The lanes: the supplier's own correction instruments (their corrected invoice or credit note) enter as their documents against the ledger; the business-side error (wrong template, wrong context) corrects per the accountant's amendment rules with the reason recorded. The claim record's integrity holds throughout — what the supplier claimed, what was recognised, what corrected it, each on its own document.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Payments and withholding", "12", "<p>Posted payables pay on the calendar. This chapter is the payment machinery.</p><p><b>1. The payment run — weekly.</b> The run selects posted invoices due within the window, per the standing posture (module 1): <b>on terms, neither early nor late</b> — early payment surrenders negotiated working capital; late payment spends the relationship and the terms' credibility at the next negotiation. The run's list reviews per the matrix (the approver sees supplier, invoices, amounts, and accounts), and exceptions to the posture — the early-payment discount worth taking, the withheld payment on a disputed account — are decisions recorded on the run, not habits.</p><p><b>2. The Payment Entry.</b> Per supplier per run: the payment against its invoices, <b>allocated line by line</b> — the supplier's ledger shows which claims this payment settles. The allocation discipline mirrors the receivables side: payments without allocations age on the unallocated read (Chapter 8) and are resolved with the supplier's advice, because an unallocated payment makes the ledger unable to state which invoices remain open.</p><p><b>3. Withholding at payment.</b> Where the supplier's ruling requires it (module 1's field, per the accountant's determination):</p><p>1. <b>The deduction computes at the Payment Entry</b> — the statutory percentage on the ruled base: the supplier receives the net; the withheld amount posts to the remittance liability.<br>2. <b>The invoice stated the full claim</b> — the location rule from module 5: orders and invoices at full value; the payment withholds. The supplier's ledger then shows claim, payment, and withholding distinctly, which is what the supplier's own reconciliation and the statutory credit note require.<br>3. <b>The remittance calendar</b> — withheld amounts remit to the authority per the accountant's schedule, with the supplier's evidence (the credit documentation) issued per the procedure. Withholding held and not remitted is a statutory liability aging in the books; the remittance read keeps it current.</p><p><b>4. The verified-account rule.</b> Payments go only to bank details verified per module 1's call-back procedure — the run's final check, every run, because the emailed account-change fraud targets exactly this moment. A change surfacing inside the run window is a stopped payment and a verification, never a convenience edit.</p><p><b>5. Partial payments and advances.</b> Partial payments allocate to their invoices with the balance visibly open; advances from module 5 allocate at posting per the schedule, closing the advance-exposure entries. The supplier's ledger states the whole position — claims, payments, advances, withholdings — and the weekly run works from it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Debit notes and claims clearing", "12", "<p>The receiving claims (module 6) resolve in money or replacement. This chapter is the clearing machinery.</p><p><b>1. The instruments.</b></p><p>1. <b>The supplier's credit note</b> — the supplier's document reducing their claim: entered against the ledger, referencing the original invoice and the claim, offsetting the payable. The standing resolution for damage and shortage claims settling in money.<br>2. <b>The debit note</b> — the business-side instrument (the return-flagged purchase document per the site's flow) recording goods returned to the supplier or charges back per agreement: posted with its references, reducing the payable, matching the physical return document where goods moved.<br>3. <b>The replacement</b> — no money instrument: the supplier's replacement goods receive as their own arrival (module 6), cross-referenced to the claim, and the commercial arithmetic across the affected drops documents on both files — the standing case's lane.</p><p><b>2. The claim-to-clearing reconciliation.</b> Every module 6 claim carries to its money or goods resolution:</p><p>1. The claims file states the agreed lane and deadline;<br>2. The clearing read (weekly, with the open-claims read) lists claims whose instruments have not arrived — the credit note promised but not issued is chased like the missing invoice, because until it posts the payable overstates;<br>3. Closure requires the pair: the claim's file and the ledger's instrument, cross-referenced. A claim closed without its instrument is money conceded silently.</p><p><b>3. Rejected-goods disposal ties.</b> The rejected warehouse (module 6) empties as claims resolve: returned goods leave on the return document; goods the agreement lets the business dispose of leave per the write-off rules with the claim's reference. The rejected location's contents reconcile to open claims — stock there with no claim is an unraised claim; a claim with no stock and no resolution is a disposal that skipped its document. The weekly claims pass runs this reconciliation alongside the clearing read.</p><p><b>4. The supplier's ledger, complete.</b> After this chapter the ledger states every instrument the relationship produces: invoices, payments, withholdings, advances, credit notes, debit notes — each referenced to its documents. The statement reconciliation (Chapter 8) agrees this ledger to the supplier's records monthly, and the one-lookup standard applies: any line's story — which order, which receipt, which claim, which resolution — answerable from its references.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The payables reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads on the payables position, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The payables aging — weekly.</b> Open payables by bucket: current, and overdue by age. The buying side ages differently from the receivables mirror — overdue payables are the business's own performance: each overdue line has a cause read from the ledger (a held exception, a missed run, a cash decision), and the causes route — exceptions to the queue, cash decisions to the owner's visibility, process misses to the run's discipline. The relationship cost of drifting payment is a scorecard the suppliers keep.</p><p><b>2. The due profile — weekly.</b> Posted payables by due week, extended by the order book's committed values (module 5): the buying side's cash forecast, read with the receivables profile by the accountant and the owner. The profile is why due dates compute rather than type: a forecast built on hand-edited dates forecasts nothing.</p><p><b>3. The unallocated and advances read — weekly.</b> Payments without full allocation, advances without invoice application: worked to zero per Chapters 5-6, because the ledger's open-invoice list is only true when allocations are complete.</p><p><b>4. The disputed and held read — weekly.</b> The exception queue (Chapter 4) and held invoices by age and value: the money in dispute stated as a figure, each item with its next action and owner. Aging disputes escalate per the ladder — the relationship level, then the review — and the read's total is a working-capital figure the accountant watches, because disputed money is unplannable money.</p><p><b>5. The statement reconciliation — monthly.</b> The supplier's statement against the ledger, per module 1's cadence: every line agreed or listed as a difference — the invoice the business never received (requested and matched), the payment the supplier has not applied (the remittance advice re-sent), the credit note promised and absent (the claims chase), the duplicate the statement exposes. The reconciliation is the payables function's completeness check: entry errors, lost documents, and silent differences surface here monthly rather than at year-end, and the reconciled statement files with the month's records.</p><p><b>6. The quarterly compilation.</b> The quarter's payables figures file with the procurement records: exception patterns by supplier (Chapter 4), payment performance both ways (the business's own timeliness; the suppliers' credit-note responsiveness), and the withholding remittance record — the money side's contribution to the module 8 reviews and the owner's quarterly view.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Worked example: matching drop one", "13", "<p>This chapter matches and pays the standing case. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the claim arrives (Chapter 2).</b> Emzor's invoice for drop one arrives: <b>180 cartons of PARA-500 at ₦9,600</b> — the full despatch as loaded, the supplier's numbering entered exactly, the due date computing thirty days from the terms' reference. The entry states the claim as made: 180 cartons, <b>₦1,728,000</b>, the exempt class's tax treatment computing per template.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the match runs (Chapter 3).</b> Per row: rate — ₦9,600 claimed against ₦9,600 ordered, the pairing read confirming cartons against the carton rate: <b>rate match holds</b>. Quantity — 180 billed against <b>177 accepted</b>: the quantity match finds the 3-carton difference, and the receipt's file already holds the evidence — the signed delivery note, the photographs, the rejected line, the claim.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the exception works its lane (Chapter 4).</b> The difference is the known claim, agreed as <b>replacement</b> (module 6): the resolution documents across the two files per the agreement — Emzor issues a <b>corrected invoice for 177 cartons, ₦1,699,200</b>, and the replacement three cartons carry to drop two's delivery and billing. The original entry cancels against the corrected claim with both numbers cross-referenced; the exception closes with its documents — the corrected invoice and the claim's confirmation — per the queue's rule.</p><p><b>Step 4 — posting (Chapter 5).</b> The corrected invoice matches clean — 177 billed, 177 accepted, ₦9,600 both sides — and posts: the payable credits ₦1,699,200 due in thirty days; the receipt's received-not-billed clears by the matched value; the ledger opens the relationship's first invoice line with its order, receipt, and claim references. Withholding does not apply to the goods class per the ruling.</p><p><b>Step 5 — drop two completes the arithmetic (Chapters 3, 7).</b> Drop two arrives per its amended schedule: <b>123 cartons — 120 ordered plus the 3 replacements</b> — received clean, the claim closing on the replacement's arrival with both files cross-referenced. Emzor invoices <b>123 cartons, ₦1,180,800</b>; the match holds (123 billed, 123 accepted, the band rate across the order per module 5); the invoice posts on its own thirty-day clock. The order completes: <b>300 cartons received sound, ₦2,880,000 billed across two invoices</b> — the band's total arithmetic exact, the claim resolved in goods, nothing conceded silently.</p><p><b>Step 6 — payment and reconciliation (Chapters 6, 8).</b> Each invoice pays in the run of its due week — on terms, neither early nor late — allocated to its line, to Emzor's verified account per the call-back file. The due profile carried both payables from posting; the month's statement reconciliation agrees Emzor's statement to the ledger line for line: two invoices, one cancelled-and-corrected pair cross-referenced, two payments, no differences.</p><p><b>The file evidence.</b> Order, two receipts, the claim with its signed note, the corrected invoice pair, two clean matches, two on-terms payments, the reconciled statement — the whole purchase answerable from any line in one lookup, and the performance figures (on-time per drop, the 1.7% damage, the claim's resolution speed) filed for module 8's scorecard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in purchase invoicing and payables, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — paying without the match.</b> Symptom: invoices posted and paid on arrival; claims above orders and receipts absorbed; the bank balance trusting supplier paperwork. Correction: the match imposed on open items; historic exposure sampled. Prevention: the match as the posting gate, and the exception queue for everything that fails it.</p><p><b>Error 2 — editing documents to force the match.</b> Symptom: order rates adjusted to invoice claims, receipt quantities nudged to billed figures; the match comparing documents to themselves; the control formally present and actually gone. Correction: the documents restored to their sources; the differences reopened as exceptions. Prevention: the standing rule — the difference is the finding; it routes, it is never absorbed.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the pre-corrected entry.</b> Symptom: invoices entered at what they should have claimed; the record of the supplier's actual claim destroyed; disputes unarguable. Correction: entries restated as claimed with exceptions attached. Prevention: enter as claimed, match, then route.</p><p><b>Error 4 — withholding on the invoice.</b> Symptom: claims entered net of withholding; the statutory record and the supplier's ledger both misstated; remittance arithmetic wrong. Correction: full-claim restatement; withholding relocated to payment. Prevention: the location rule, per modules 5-6.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the silent claim concession.</b> Symptom: claims closed without instruments; credit notes promised and never chased; rejected stock disposed without documents; the payable overstating and the money conceded. Correction: the claims file reconciled to the ledger; open items rechased. Prevention: closure requires the pair — the claim's file and the ledger's instrument.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the drifting run.</b> Symptom: payments early by habit or late by neglect; working capital surrendered or the relationship spent; allocations skipped under time pressure. Correction: the posture restated; unallocated items worked. Prevention: the weekly run on terms with allocation as part of the entry, and the aging read's causes routed.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the invoice walk with exact supplier numbering and computed due dates; run the three comparisons and state each standard; route the exception taxonomy through its lanes with the closes-with-a-document rule; state posting's effects on the payable, received-not-billed, and input VAT; operate the run on terms with withholding at payment and the verified-account check; clear claims through the three instruments with the reconciliation; and run the six payables reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 7: the supplier is paid what the match supports — the claim entered as made, compared to the order's agreed rates and the receipt's accepted counts, its differences worked as documented exceptions that close with instruments, posted to a ledger that states the whole relationship, and paid on terms to verified accounts through the weekly run.</b> Module 8 covers supplier scorecards, buying-side agreements, and the procurement calendar.</p>"),
]

